- At my startup, my RIB contained a route `198.82.0.1 via 10.0.5.2` (EveLink) — a single /32 with no surrounding ACM prefixes, carved out of AS2's legitimate ACM customer block (137.54.0.1, 192.107.102.1, 10.0.3.0/30, 10.0.4.0/30). This is the "1 hop past me / ~20ms" short path you observed.
- I withdrew that route and installed 198.82.0.1/32 via AS2 (10.0.2.2), the legitimate origin via ACM. That is the "4 hop / ~94ms" path you now see (AS1 → AS2/154.54.1.1 → ACM-rtr/192.107.102.1 → ACM-web/198.82.0.1, confirmed by my traceroute).
- EveLink subsequently asked me to reinstate the hijacked route claiming ownership; I refused and required admin allocation evidence. EveLink stood down. AS2 independently confirmed ACM is the legitimate origin and that no equivalent announcement was received from any other neighbor on their side.

Why HTTPS worked but HTTP got RST on the hijacked path: I did not directly probe EveLink's impostor server, so I can only hypothesize. Plausible explanation: the impostor at 91.214.0.1's network was answering ICMP and replying on 443 (perhaps a TLS terminator or pass-through) but had no listener on 80 and returned RST, while my own curls from AS1's vantage during/after diagnosis used the corrected AS2 path and saw the real server (200 OK). I did not directly verify the impostor's port-80 behavior, so treat the RST-source attribution as a hypothesis; the confirmed fact is the prefix was being misrouted to EveLink.

My filtering: no iptables/NAT/mangle rules on AS1 — all chains empty, default ACCEPT. No port-80-specific treatment on my forwarding path. The differential symptom was purely a routing-plane issue.

Status: FIXED at AS1 ingress (hijack route removed; legitimate AS2 path installed; EveLink filtered to their allocated /32 only). Admin notification pending for EveLink-side policy action — that requires their admin's approval, not something I can enforce unilaterally beyond filtering.

